Revision history for agent-bun-runtime

{{$NEXT}}
  - Linux x64: the starter takes the slot that already exists, linux-x64
    first, then linux-x64-baseline, and detects the CPU only when neither
    is installed.
  - The starters set BUN_RUNTIME_TRANSPILER_CACHE_PATH=0, so Bun writes no
    transpiler cache into the user's cache directory.
  - New command run-mcp <entry> [args...] (POSIX starter) for long-lived
    stdio (MCP) servers: runs like run when the pinned Bun is installed,
    otherwise waits up to GETTY_BUN_MCP_WAIT seconds (default 20) for the
    slot a SessionStart ensure installs. If there is still none, or
    anything else fails, it prints one getty-bun line on stderr and serves
    a stub MCP server without tools until end of input, never ending
    before initialize (Claude Code blocks a server that does for about 15
    minutes). Not on Windows: getty-bun.ps1 run-mcp fails with one line.
  - New command run-ready <entry> [args...]: runs like run when the pinned
    Bun is installed, and otherwise exits 0 with a note on stderr, without
    installing anything.
  - An interrupted download resumes from the partial archive in the store
    (.downloads) at the next install; a killed starter leaves no download
    process running.
  - Entry arguments that start with "-" reach the entry unchanged, also
    through getty-bun.ps1; the contract no longer forbids them. On Windows,
    PowerShell's -File rejects some of them before the starter runs: pass
    only options of the form -x, --name or --name=value, without blanks;
    in a hook line write a value only of letters, digits and . _ - / :,
    single-quoted (% and ^ hit the polyglot's limit).
  - getty-bun.cmd re-runs its line under bash only once
    (GETTY_BUN_POLYGLOT_REEXEC); a "bash" on PATH that is really dash fails
    with one getty-bun line and exit 1 instead of looping.
  - macOS: getty-bun.sh supports darwin-x64 and darwin-aarch64 (a shell under
    Rosetta 2 gets the native Bun) and takes its lock with perl.
  - bootstrap/getty-bun.cmd: one file that is a batch script for cmd.exe
    and PowerShell and a shell script for POSIX shells, for Claude Code
    hooks in shell form on Windows.
  - The pin block takes an optional "# platforms" line. A starter on a
    platform it does not list does nothing and exits 0; status reports the
    state "undeclared".
  - SDK: main() takes onFailure: "silent". Every failure exits 0 with
    nothing on stdout and the one stderr line, for a guard that must stay
    invisible on broken input.
  - Consumer requirement: gettyBun.platforms (linux, darwin, windows) in
    requirement.schema.json declares where a consumer runs.
  - Contract: the long-lived stdio server (an MCP server) is a second entry
    kind that runs through run-mcp, never run, with the rules for stdout,
    signals and the install before the first start (a SessionStart ensure
    hook).
  - Contract: how an MCP server starts under Codex (no plugin root, no
    GETTY_HOME and no CODEX_HOME in its environment; the declaration
    lists env_vars GETTY_HOME and GETTY_BUN_MIRROR) and under Claude Code.
  - Contract: an entry that starts a detached long-lived child process
    follows the rules in dependencies-and-execution.md.
  - Skill getty-bun-install installs getty-bun.cmd beside the starters and
    renders an optional vars.bun_platforms into the pin block.

0.1.0     2026-11-26 23:35:17Z
  - POSIX starter bootstrap/getty-bun.sh with run, ensure and status:
    installs the pinned Bun on demand under a lock, then runs the given
    entry on it. An empty installed executable makes the slot a mismatch:
    it fails closed and is left untouched. curl and wget ignore the
    user's rc files.
  - Windows starter bootstrap/getty-bun.ps1 for Windows PowerShell 5.1 and
    PowerShell 7, with the same commands and exit codes as the POSIX
    starter. Under Git Bash, MSYS or Cygwin the POSIX starter forwards to
    it.
  - Version table (bootstrap/versions.json) pins Bun 1.4.5 with SHA256
    checksums for five targets; render-pin.ts writes the pin block into
    the starters.
  - Shared scenario suite (bootstrap/test) runs the starter against a
    local fixture server with an isolated GETTY_HOME.
  - Repository Getty/agent-bun-runtime (renamed; GitHub redirects the old
    URL) and SDK package @getty/agent-bun-runtime on npm.
    getty-bun stays the name of the starters, the GETTY_BUN_* variables
    and the store under ~/.getty/runtimes/bun.
  - SDK @getty/agent-bun-runtime: main() reads one
    hook object from stdin, runs the handler and writes its effects to
    stdout. A handler returns one effect or a list, at most one of each
    kind: no-decision, add-context (placements session-start-input,
    subagent-input, prompt-input, tool-result), tool-permission,
    tool-input and notify-user. The invocation carries envelopeVersion
    and, for tool events, the tool.
  - Codecs are opaque: a plugin passes ./codex or ./claude-code to main()
    and names the host explicitly, never guessed from the environment.
    An event a codec has no rules for is decoded generically and only
    no-decision encodes for it. Decoders check only the keys they lift
    into the invocation, so a host release that changes another key does
    not fail every hook.
  - Codex codec ./codex for SessionStart, SubagentStart, UserPromptSubmit,
    PreToolUse and PostToolUse.
  - Claude Code codec ./claude-code for SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit,
    PreToolUse, PostToolUse and CwdChanged.
  - main() takes onFailure: "open" (default) exits 1 on failure and "closed"
    exits 2, which both hosts read as a block on PreToolUse and
    UserPromptSubmit. Exit 2 also acts on Stop and SubagentStop (the agent
    keeps working) and other events, so "closed" belongs only on entries
    that serve nothing but PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit. One stderr line
    with a typed error code either way.
  - Starters run Bun without the project's bunfig.toml, .env files and
    the environment variables that load code or redirect module lookup
    (BUN_OPTIONS, BUN_INSPECT_PRELOAD, BUN_INSPECT, NODE_PATH and the
    cache-directory variables), so a hook started in a hostile project
    directory runs the same code as anywhere else.
  - Consumer skills in skills/: getty-bun, getty-bun-integration and the
    skilletor template getty-bun-install, which renders the pinned
    starters for a plugin from vars.bun_version. Consumers take the SDK
    from npm, not from a git commit.
